    "turn_text": "happening in America. Welcome into the Flight Politics Podcast. I'm your host, Tyler Page. Got a great show for you today. Representative Chris Stewart presents the State of Utah in addition. We're going to talk about the presidential election. What are the questions that actually matter and what doesn't? Four questions that will decide the presidential race in our in-depth topic. But let's go ahead and get started. There's a lot to talk about today. Obviously, the biggest story, the disaster area that is Baltimore. There's been rioting. It all follows the death of a young man by the name of Freddie Gray. Freddie was taken into custody by police, and during his time in custody by police, he sustained injuries that eventually killed him. Freddie was a young African American male, and combining that with police incidents that have happened across the country over the course of the last few months and years led to rioting, looting, and all sorts of problems. The mayor made a really unfortunate statement, quote unquote, space to destroy. She apologized for that, walked it back. But to some degree, the damage was done. And Fox News found police sources that she told police to stand down and allow them to do that. Now, look. I think in all of these cases, and by the way, this underlies our biggest problem when it comes to the world of politics in general. We just need empathy and understanding that everybody's life experiences are not our own and the ability to respect other people who have different life experiences. I think that alone, if everybody had it, would solve 99% of the way we talk about politics. People are still going to disagree and that's okay. But coming to understand that people have different experiences and because they have those different experiences, they have different opinions, that's part of life. The reality is, police do a lot of good things and they do some problematic things in the way they handle their jobs. When you're talking about a huge white police force, there are going to be bad eggs that do bad things, there are going to be good eggs, and there are going to be policies that target specific groups. and you know we're trying to eliminate that on a racial basis but the reality is if you just look at age police are never going to scrutinize an 80 year old woman the same way they do a 20 year old man within any race on a racial basis though we try to get the police to stop but here's my point and I'm not trying to advocate for racial profiling or anything like that at its core people in suburban white communities probably need to have a little more empathy in looking at those in minority communities where they feel like they've been a little more targeted by police. On the other side people who get upset with those white suburbanites need to realize that their life experience is not the same as those in those communities. There is a desire for everybody to be treated the same and you know when I talk to everyone in this country. Everyone, I've yet to meet a person who said to me, while I really wish members of X-race would be treated differently than members of Y-race. Everybody wants everybody to be treated the same and fairly. What we're still figuring out is some of the nuances",
