    "turn_text": "and how that operates in a world where certain races, namely Latinos, African Americans, are disproportionately less wealthy than whites and Asian Americans. And that's going to influence how police address things and poverty rate influences the crime rate. And so how all that works out, and those are difficult nuances to go through, but I think if everybody has a little more empathy, it sits back and understands, just says, how would I feel, okay, can I have some empathy? I don't have to agree, but can I have some empathy? I think that solves a lot of our problems. Democrats talk about a need for a racial conversation. President Obama calls for more funding of different projects and to some degree played the race card on Congress saying that He had no illusion that they would oppose funding for urban areas, which was kind of code for they won't help black people. But it is the attitude that some have. Some have pointed out that there's not really any proposals he has that would touch that. The response from Republicans has been to point out that Democrats run Baltimore. They haven't had a Republican mayor since the 1960s. They've only had two since the 1940s. They have what is kind of a Democratic Party wish list of spending. They have perennially been in the top five highest education funding per pupil in among all the large school districts in the country. So if you look at that, there's been plenty of funding as what Republicans are saying. and Democrats run it and so that racist mayor and that racist police chief are both African Americans and they're Democrats. The mayor noted last year that incidents of police brutality were down as were complaints and yet this is still going on. I think when we look at this problem what we need to step back and say is that number one people need to have empathy and then I'll let you decide on the politics. How much of this is Republicans? Democrats will say that Republicans nationally don't put help into these cities. Republicans point out that Democrats run Baltimore and that frankly they've gotten a lot of what they wanted in Baltimore and yet these are still the problem. I want to finish off this thing, this story, with a new segment that highlights when media members behave in ways that make our country a worse place. I like to call it you are the problem.",
