    "turn_text": "You are the problem, of course. This week is going to get awarded to Salon and the nation. Both of them have called for violence and encouraged the violence to continue. And that's just so problematic. Do we need to have a discussion? Absolutely. Particularly about policing methods in America. And I think in a broader sense, the way our public officials treat citizens. It's not just police that gets to the IRS, that gets to the Justice Department, that gets to all sorts of groups, we need to have that discussion about how that role and that relationship should operate. However, advocating violence as a solution is never a good option. These two outlets are known for frankly, some irresponsible behavior, but I wanted to point out and say to the folks that salon in the nation, you are the problem. Turn now to another story here. The Supreme Court heard oral arguments this week on whether to legalize gay marriage nationally. It's currently legalized in 37 states. And the Supreme Court is going to legalize this nationally. Now, how they do it matters and when they read their decision, we'll take more time to discuss it. But the reality is, because of the way this has been approached, the voting public has no say on this issue. And so I don't see a whole lot of reasons to discuss this too much. The decision will come in late June and gay marriage should be legalized nationally at that point. Last story, I wanted to touch on the Clinton Foundation. Just quickly, some things came out this week that I think were worthy of discussion, and so this is an update on the previous story. First, I came out that former President Clinton gave 13 speeches that were paid for by companies and governments with business before the State Department at that time, while Hillary Clinton was Secretary of State, and that included $2.5 million to him. various studies showed between six and fifteen percent of clinton foundation funds actually went to what are called programmatic grants that's how most charities do their charitable work is they give money to people right so but in their disclosures only six to fifteen percent of the money depending on what year range you're looking at actually goes to that now the clinton's have pushed back the clinton foundation i should say is pushed back and said look We actually hire employees who actually do charitable work as opposed to just giving out grants and being a pass-through group. A lot of charities when you give them money, they just pass the money through to someone else. In this case, the Clinton Foundation says they actually have hired staff who do that, and they'll put numbers closer. The 80 to 88% of our funding goes to charitable work. I think the answer is probably somewhere between those numbers, and it's going to get into a few things. The biggest is what constitutes charitable work. The Clintons, two biggest charities are the Clinton Global Initiative. and the Bill Clinton Presidential Library. Bill Clinton Presidential Library, I guess you could call it a charity, but it's not really that focused on helping to make people's lives better. It's focused on promoting promoting the former president, which is fine and it's a non-profit type situation, but it's not really a charitable endeavor. The Clinton Global Initiative, the filing I saw was $23 million this last year. It is a gigantic party in which they bring in global leaders from around the world, heads of state, celebrities, et cetera. And they meet to discuss issues related to charity. And the idea from the Clinton side is we're going to bring powerful people together and get them agree to help do things to make the world a better place. And they claim hundreds of millions of people have benefited from this. Some Clinton critics point out that this is just a big part and that's all it really is. So they would argue that's not a charitable endeavor. Here's the point. I'm going to touch on a couple stories, a couple points here that I think are worth mentioning. The Sunlight Foundation, which monitors charities and has kind of a pro-democrat bent, has called it a slush fund for the Clintons, pays to fly them around the world and do whatever they want to do. It has, by some allegations, been paying to fly Hillary Clinton around to get political speeches back before she announced her candidacy for president. I'll let you decide and dig into that, but that's the allegation. Politico reported this week President Clinton and Chelsea Clinton are taking donors on a safari. President Clinton does these trips around the world and he invites people to come with him and it's usually a reward for donating sometimes to political side. So that is a story. Now look, why does this keep coming up and why does it matter? First, Hillary Clinton is not really meeting with the media, so they're going to continue to dig into this stuff. She says she's a presidential candidate, and yet she's answered as of this last Monday seven questions as a presidential candidate for media. The media is going to try and dig in. They're going to do everything they can to get information. When somebody says they're a candidate for president, the media starts combing through their garbage and everything else. You can talk about whether that's a good thing or a bad thing, but the reality is that's the treatment that everyone gets. And almost every other candidate is meeting with media, if not daily, very close to daily, and taking specific questions from those media members. And Hillary's not. And because she's not, They're going to continue to dig and cover however they can. That concludes our first segment. When we come back, it's time to talk about the four questions that will decide the presidential election. Thanks for downloading the podcast. Be sure to like the polite politics community fan page on Facebook and be sure to tell your friends. Welcome back. As we head into this presidential election, There's a lot of media coverage that is just a joke. They focus on, sometimes people call it the horse race coverage. People focus on a lot of really silly things and outside stories. I want to touch on the things that are actually going to decide which party and who wins the presidential election. I've got four questions, two related to the Democrats, two related to the Republicans. And I think these pretty objectively will decide who wins the presidential election. Question number one. Will Democrats nominate Hillary Clinton as their candidate? This seems very probable. John Stewart did a segment this week, pointing out that nobody else is gaining a ton of traction. The only candidate I see that really potentially could is Martin O'Malley, who is the former governor of the state of Maryland and immensely unpopular. However, he's very progressive and could appeal to the left wing of the Democratic Party. Hillary Clinton seems to be afraid of the left wing of the Democratic Party. And so she is moving that direction saying things like we need to topple the 1%. But in general, she keeps trying to say things that make her sound more progressive or more far left. And People from that, the progressive wing of the Democratic Party, generally say, we don't believe you. We know who you are. You're not the progressive wing of the Democratic Party. Look, Hillary is likely to win the nomination. If I were putting odds, I'd put it at about 75% right now. However, you need to understand that if she loses Iowa, this thing could fall apart overnight. The reality with Secretary Clinton is that she's inevitable because she's inevitable. And if a chink appears in that armor, that support is going to fade overnight. As soon as somebody decides that somebody else is a viable alternative, this is going to change really fast. So the question is whether Democrats will decide that or not. If they don't, they're all supporting Hillary because they're all supporting Hillary. But as soon as somebody doesn't think they're supporting Hillary anymore, the like for Hillary Clinton is actually not that high among the Democratic Party in terms of their ideal candidate. If Democrats nominate somebody who is not Hillary Clinton, the chance of that person winning the White House are almost zero. It could happen, but I think it's really unlikely. Question number two, will Hillary be able to replicate her husband, Bill, or President Obama's coalition in the election? There are challenges to both and there are benefits to both. Bill's coalition that allowed him to become president was heavy on blue-collar working-class whites. But these are the same people who have abandoned President Obama and the Democratic Party over the last eight years. In addition, Hillary is not Bill. That seems like an obvious point. Bill Clinton is one of the most charismatic, talented politicians of a generation. Hillary Clinton is not a good candidate. She's not a great speaker. She doesn't inspire people when she speaks in the same way that Bill or President Obama did. President Obama's coalition is largely built on his personality and who he is. His percentages among minority voters were much higher in 2008 and 2012 than his standard for Democrats to win. He won a higher percentage of minority voters. Will Hillary be able to do that even though she isn't a minority? Well, in elections where President Obama hasn't been on the ballot, in midterm elections where he's campaigned for people. That coalition hasn't held as tightly with the Democratic nominee. Moreover, President Obama's policies, when you name his policies and ask voters what they think of them, they aren't particularly popular. President Obama, the person, is popular. So the question is whether that will transfer as well. Finally, and I mentioned this before, for voters over 40, all of this news about Hillary Clinton's funding machine, not gonna matter at all, because everybody knew this sort of stuff was part of the Clintons. The problem is President Obama lost everyone over 30 years old in 2012. If you took the 2012 election and just took people over 30, Mitt Romney would have won. It's that younger group, that really, really helped him. And that group, frankly, has had 16 years where presidents don't do the sort of things that the Clintons do. And so if those young people are willing to accept that, is an open question. And that could have trouble for replicating President Obama's coalition as well. That said, I think Hillary does have a good chance to create her own coalition, which would probably be a combination of aspects of these two. Now to the Republican candidates. Number one, will Republicans nominate an electable candidate? As my Democrat friends tell me, President Obama won his two elections by default. The 2008 election, the economy had just collapsed. John McCain is not well liked in Republican circles. So that one seemed like didn't have much of a chance. And then Mitt Romney said some things and made it hard for him to identify with a lot of people. And a lot of white male voters didn't turn up in the 2012 election. I think the key here is that whoever the candidate is, they have to appeal to low information voters and the center of the American public. They can't just be able to talk to Republicans. They have to be able to speak to independents as well. And they have to be able to win independents by a decent margin. When Republicans do that, they win almost everywhere. When they don't, they lose almost everywhere. There is disagreement in Republican circles over whether that means they need somebody whose policies are more centrist or whether they need someone who can clearly articulate their views and their vision of the world. I'm not going to take a stance on that. I think different people have different opinions. What I am going to say is whoever it is needs to be able to win over independent voters and low information voters because that's where Republicans have really struggled. The second question related to Republicans is similar, and that is, can the Republican brand rebound? The Republican brand is really, really poor right now. If I were sitting in front of Ryan's previous, who was the chair of the Republican National Committee, I would be screaming at him to go on a nationwide branding campaign, identifying what Republicans are and what Republicans aren't. Is he willing to do that? Probably not. However, he has spent some money doing minority outreach and other things like that that I think will help the Republican Party. However, the party brand is really toxic, especially among voters who make less than $50,000 a year and low information voters. The Republican Party has to improve its brand standing at least a little if it wants to have a chance at winning the presidential election. So those are the four questions. First, will the Democrats nominate Hillary Clinton? Will Hillary be able to replicate Bill or Obama's coalition? Will Republicans nominate an electable candidate? And can the Republican brand rebound? These are the questions that are really going to decide the 2000 and... 16 election. It's not going to be the stupid little horse race stuff here and there. Anything that refers back to these four questions I would say pay attention to, anything that doesn't, I wouldn't worry about it because frankly it won't be relevant. When we come back, Congressman Chris Stewart took time to sit down and talk with me about a lot of things including how much time Congressman work and a lot of other issues so I think he'll enjoy it. Hey, everyone. Thanks for downloading the podcast. Do you disagree with something I said? You're probably not alone. Shoot me an email at tpage at politepolitics.com. And if you make a good point, I'll read it on the air.",
