  {
    "episode_id": 90236,
    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/9/0/90236.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 8,
    "turn_text": "Oh, gosh, you know, people actually ask that all the time. And, you know, I had a great life. I was actually very happy, and I really was not a kind of person who ever thought about You know, one day I'm going to run for office. You know, like you said, I was in the military for a long time. I was a successful author and really just quite happy. But like a lot of Americans, as I sat and watched what was happening, I just reached the point where I couldn't stand it anymore. And it really was a lot of the same process that I went through when I decided to join the Air Force out of college. I was planning to go to law school. In fact, it started to apply to a lot of schools, and in my senior year of college, I thought, I want to serve in the military. I want to have that experience. And I want to serve like my dad did, and my brothers have done. And when it came to this office, it was, like I said, much the same experience. And Utah was allocated an additional seat. And someone called me one morning and said, they just voted on the boundaries. And you're in an open district. And you should run for Congress. And I just thought that's what I want to do. That's how I think I can help. And now here we",
