    "episode_id": 90236,
    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/9/0/90236.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 14,
    "turn_text": "question. You know, one thing I think that people would be interested in is just the sheer number of bills that we consider. I think the last Congress, there was something more than 6,000 bills that were introduced in the House of Representatives. And there's just no possible way that you can keep your arms or, you know, wrap your arms around that, that number, that amount of legislation. So they kind of get filtered, though. I mean, some of them make it to the floor, some of them don't. And some of these bills are really quite easy. You read them and you go, okay, that's an easy one for me. I know I support that or I know I don't support that. But there are some, like you said Tyler, that a little harder because there's parts of it that I think are actually very important, that there might be other parts that I'm concerned with or I don't think is a good idea. Sometimes those bills change in the amendment process on the floor and they're either made better or some cases are made worse and that may make the decision easier. But every once in a while, I mean, you really have a gut check. You really think, you know, this is a very difficult vote for me because on the whole, I could go either way, that there are some good things and some bad things. And there's been two or three times, so my hand is actually hovered over the yes, no button as I've tried to decide. And time is running out on the vote. You know, you only have a few minutes to vote, and then they close the vote. There's been a couple of times when I just really went back and forth trying",
