  {
    "episode_id": 90236,
    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/9/0/90236.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 20,
    "turn_text": "Well, I think the Tea Party is kind of, you know, It's the perception of it has changed a little bit over the years. I mean, originally, it's original intent was, as was I, watching our debt and our spending, you just looked at that and go, this is absolutely insane. And in fact, that is the primary reason that I decided to run for Congress. Because I felt like we could destroy our country if we don't get a handle on our debt. And we can't spend money like we were and have $1.4 trillion annual deficits and just pretend that that's okay. And I think that was a real genesis for a lot of the emotion and a lot of the original inception of the Tea Party was a lot of people feeling that way. But like a lot of movements, they've kind of changed and migrated a little bit since then. I had a lot of Tea Party support. I don't think I could have won had I not had Tea Party and other real conservatives like myself if I had not had their support. So, and you know, the fact that the Tea Party has brought the conversation around to some of these important issues, I just think that's very important. But I didn't run as a Tea Party candidate. I ran as a conservative Republican, and that's kind of where my heart is.",
